      "writtenOn": "2023-02-13T21:21:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "John\u0027s is already idiomatic in Rust. Its function style allows us to avoid intermediate mutables which is preferred. It\u0027s a bit verbose because Rust currently does not have a way to collect from iterator directly to sized array, hence the `collect::Vec`, `try_into`, and `match` steps.\n\nSince we find using this very often, I recommend we generalize this into a function:\n\n```\nfn to_sized_array\u003cconst S: usize\u003e(v: \u0026Vec\u003cu8\u003e) -\u003e [u8; S] {\n    v.iter().chain(std::iter::repeat(\u00260)).take(S).cloned().collect::\u003cVec\u003cu8\u003e\u003e().try_into().unwrap()\n}\n```\nAnd the usage can be simply:\n```\nbtpin.pin \u003d to_sized_array::\u003c16\u003e(\u0026pin_code);\n```\n\nUsing the `S` const generic guarantees at compile time that the size in `take` matches the array size, so that we can use naked `unwrap()` after `try_into()` safely.",
      "parentUuid": "ee2a2adb_2bd09ba1",
      "revId": "b14ae94e637a7b464885d6ff077a472da5df77bd",
